ied for my end  of high school exams got a decent score  and got accepted into a good university  how was i able to do that was it because  i worked hard and wasn't lazy to some  extent sure i worked hard and wasn't  lazy in my own private room based on  instructions by good teachers using high  quality textbooks with supportive  parents after years of professional help  during my childhood to eliminate my  learning disabilities while eating  healthy nutritious meals in a house with  electricity and running water compare  this to say a roma teenager living in  eastern hungary at the age of town in  deep poverty who really wants to get  into university like i did they study  hard in a crowded room based on  instructions by overworked unmotivated  teachers using substandard out of date  textbooks with unsupported parents with  learning disabilities that were never  corrected while eating cheap low-quality  meals in a house with barely electricity  and possibly no running water so suppose  both the roma teenager and i achieved  the same score on the end of high school  exam so the million dollar question is  did the two of us have to work just as  hard to achieve the same score like in a  true meritocracy  no absolutely not the roma teenager had  to work much much harder by an order of  magnitude that's because they had to  compensate for the substandard  circumstances they were born in and  those can only be eliminated with one  thing  wealth wealth is the cheat code to life  do we recall the 2019 college admissions  bribery scandal where it turned out that  a bunch of rich people celebrities ceos  and so on have been paying large sums of  money to get their kids into top  universities that's not too much talker  tech or let's take amazon their average  employee made twenty nine thousand seven  dollars in 2020 while jeff bezos made 70  billion dollars now do you think jeff  bezos worked 2 million for 13 210.6  times harder than the average employee  to earn that money